import Aff from '../components/Aff.astro';
import { S } from '../data/sources.js';
const title = 'Switching Virtual Mailbox Locations (or When Yours Closes): New Form 1583, Overlap, and Old Mail';
const description = 'Moving to a different address at the same provider usually means a new account and a new Form 1583, and USPS won\'t forward mail from one mailbox to another. Rules at US Global Mail, Traveling Mailbox, Virtual Post Mail and iPostal1, plus what happened when a location closed. Checked Nov 28, 2026.';
const schema = {"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"Article","headline":title,"description":description,"dateModified":"2026-11-28"};
---
<Base title={title} description={description} updated="2026-11-28" schema={schema}>
  <h1>Switching virtual mailbox locations, or when yours closes</h1>
  <div class="answer"><p><strong>Short answer:</strong> At most providers you can't just edit your address. Each address has its own Form 1583, so switching usually means opening a new mailbox at the new location, completing a new form, updating your senders yourself, and keeping the old box open until mail stops arriving. USPS rules don't allow a change-of-address order when a mailbox relationship ends, so there's no automatic forwarding from the old box.</p></div>
  <p class="lede">This applies when you move to a closer location, want a different state, or your provider moves a location. Each provider's own policy, checked November 28, 2026.</p>

  <h2>Why you can't file a change of address</h2>
  <p>USPS rules for mailbox companies (called CMRAs) say: "When the agency relationship between the CMRA and the addressee terminates, neither the addressee nor the CMRA will file a change-of-address order with the Post Office." The CMRA must instead remail your mail "for at least 6 months after the termination date", with new postage <Src href={S.dmm508} label="DMM 508.1.8" />. Virtual Post Mail says it plainly: "USPS will not allow you to file a Change of Address from one of our mailbox locations to another" <Src href={S.vpmChangeLoc} />. Anytime Mailbox: "a change of address form cannot be submitted for a digital mailbox" <Src href={S.atmRenterFaq} />.</p>

  <h2>Switching locations, provider by provider</h2>
  <div class="tablewrap"><table>
    <thead><tr><th>Provider</th><th>How a switch works</th><th>New Form 1583?</th><th>Overlap advice</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>US Global Mail</td><td>No direct change once your Form 1583 is approved. Before approval you can change the address <Src href={S.usgmChangeAddr} />. After approval: open a new account (with a different email address) at the new location <Src href={S.usgmNewLocation} /></td><td>Yes, but "if you already have a notarized Form 1583 on file, you do not need a new notary appointment" <Src href={S.usgmNewLocation} /></td><td>Keep both accounts until no more mail is coming to the old one. Closing early gets a prorated refund for unused time <Src href={S.usgmNewLocation} /></td></tr>
      <tr><td>Traveling Mailbox</td><td>Open a new account at the new address. Accounts can be linked so you switch between them without signing out <Src href={S.tmbSwitch} /></td><td>Yes, for every recipient on the new account <Src href={S.tmbSwitch1583} /></td><td>Keep the old one open until mail arrives at the new address; no fixed overlap period <Src href={S.tmbSwitchBoth} /></td></tr>
      <tr><td>Virtual Post Mail</td><td>Sign up for a new plan at the new address, then email a cancellation request for the old one. A transfer feature lets you manage both under one login <Src href={S.vpmChangeLoc} /></td><td>Yes. VPM waives the notary if you have an active, verified account, but you still provide current ID <Src href={S.vpmChangeLoc} /></td><td>Wait "at least 1-2 months" before canceling the old mailbox <Src href={S.vpmChangeLoc} /></td></tr>
      <tr><td>iPostal1</td><td>If a new location opens closer to you, "you will be able to request for your account to be transferred". You can also add locations to one account <Src href={S.ipFaq} /></td><td>Yes, "an updated notarized USPS Form 1583" <Src href={S.ipFaq} /></td><td>—</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table></div>

  <h2>Your old mail and scans don't move</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Traveling Mailbox:</strong> "Your mail and scans stay with your previous account." Download the scans you want and forward the originals you need before closing. After closure, it receives leftover mail for up to six months, and you can still sign in to request forwarding (shipping and handling charges apply) <Src href={S.tmbSwitch} />.</li>
    <li><strong>US Global Mail:</strong> mail that arrives after you close the old account "will be securely shredded" <Src href={S.usgmNewLocation} />.</li>
    <li><strong>Virtual Post Mail:</strong> the new account needs a new username, but you can transfer the mailbox to your other login in Mailbox Settings <Src href={S.vpmChangeLoc} />.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Closing the old box follows each provider's normal cancellation rules. See <a href="/cancel-virtual-mailbox/">canceling a virtual mailbox</a>.</p>

  <h2>When the provider closes or moves your location</h2>
  <p>Virtual Post Mail has published its own example. When it left its Walnut, California office, it gave customers until March 31, 2023, "about 6 months", to move to a new Covina address. The mailbox number stayed the same, staff processed mail at both addresses until the lease ended, and VPM said it now owns all the buildings its offices are in <Src href={S.vpmWalnut} />. Its disaster plan says that if a single site has to shut for days, letters may be sent to other locations for scanning (about 3 to 5 business days of delay), while packages stay on site until it reopens <Src href={S.vpmContingency} />.</p>
  <p>US Global Mail says its own facilities are in California, New York, Las Vegas and Texas. Elsewhere, physical mail is handled at "the designated location or a Channel Partner" <Src href={S.usgmProtected} />. At Anytime Mailbox and iPostal1, mail is handled by local businesses: Anytime calls them "operator mailing partners" <Src href={S.atmFaq} label="Anytime" />, and iPostal1 says most of its locations are "professional retail pack and ship stores or business coworking centers" <Src href={S.ipFaq} label="iPostal1" />. Neither page says what happens if a partner location closes, so ask before you sign up.</p>

  <h2>A switch checklist</h2>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Open the new mailbox first</strong> and get its Form 1583 approved <Src href={S.tmbSwitch1583} label="TMB" /> <Src href={S.usgmNewLocation} label="USGM" />.</li>
    <li><strong>Update senders directly</strong>: banks, insurers, the IRS, your state, subscriptions. Traveling Mailbox's list: "your bank, insurers, subscriptions and personal contacts" <Src href={S.tmbSwitch} />. For the IRS and other senders, see <a href="/change-address-to-virtual-mailbox/">changing your address</a>.</li>
    <li><strong>Overlap for a month or two.</strong> VPM suggests 1 to 2 months <Src href={S.vpmChangeLoc} /> <Src href={S.vpmSwitchService} label="VPM switching guide" />.</li>
    <li><strong>Download scans and forward originals</strong> before closing <Src href={S.tmbSwitch} />.</li>
    <li><strong>If the address is on a state filing</strong> (an LLC's principal office or mailing address), update it with the state too. See <a href="/virtual-mailbox-for-llc/">virtual mailbox for an LLC</a>.</li>
  </ol>

